    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Why Does My Audio Sound Quiet No Matter How High You Turn It Up?</h1>

    <p>
      Two audio files can technically hit the exact same maximum volume and still sound completely
      different in loudness when you actually listen to them. This isn't a bug in your player, and
      turning the volume knob up doesn't fix it — because the problem isn't about volume at all. It's
      about the difference between a file's <em>peak</em> and how loud it <em>sounds</em>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Peak is not the same thing as loudness</h2>
    <p>
      The peak of an audio file is just its single loudest moment — the tallest spike in the waveform,
      measured as a technical value. A recording of quiet, sparse acoustic guitar with one sharp pluck
      might have a peak that hits the maximum, while the rest of the track sits far quieter. A dense,
      compressed pop track might have the same technical peak, but because it's <em>consistently</em> loud
      throughout rather than mostly quiet with one spike, it sounds dramatically louder overall.
    </p>
    <p>
      Human hearing responds to sustained average loudness over time, not to a single isolated peak.
      This is exactly why "peak normalization" — raising a file until its loudest single sample hits
      the maximum — can leave two files at the same technical peak sounding wildly different in
      practice. It answers "did this file use the full available range," not "how loud does this
      actually sound."
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">What LUFS actually measures</h2>
    <p>
      The fix is a measurement called LUFS (Loudness Units Full Scale), based on a broadcast standard
      that applies psychoacoustic weighting — it accounts for the fact that human hearing is more
      sensitive to some frequencies than others, and it measures average loudness across the entire
      file rather than a single moment. Every major streaming platform (Spotify, YouTube, Apple Music)
      targets a specific LUFS value for playback, which is exactly why a quiet acoustic song and a
      loud, heavily processed pop track end up sounding roughly equally loud on the same playlist —
      the platform is normalizing to a consistent <em>perceived</em> loudness, not a consistent peak.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">What "normalize volume" actually does, then</h2>
    <p>
      A volume normalization tool measures a file's actual perceived loudness (not just its peak) and
      applies a single, uniform gain adjustment across the whole file to bring it to a target level.
      It's not compression in the audio-production sense — it doesn't change the relationship between
      the quiet and loud parts of the recording, it just shifts the whole thing up or down as one
      block until the <em>overall</em> perceived loudness lands where you want it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is why normalizing a file that already sounds appropriately loud, and re-normalizing it
      again later, doesn't meaningfully change anything — and running it repeatedly on the same file
      can very slightly alter the tonal balance for no benefit. Normalize once.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Normalizing</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/audio/volume-normalize" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">Normalize Volume</a>
      — measures the actual perceived loudness of your file and adjusts it to a consistent target,
      entirely in your browser.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-semibold text-lg pt-2 text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      If the actual problem is that one section of a longer recording is quiet relative to the rest of
      the <em>same</em> file (rather than the whole file being quiet relative to other files), normalization
      won't fix that — that's a job for actual audio editing software, not a volume adjustment across
      the whole file.
    </p>
  </article>
